Extended C Library
==================

A useful library for ANSI C99. Implements a number of data structures to
ease the development of pure C code (without needing C++, command
language, etc). Simply include the relevant header and link with the
relevant library.

On Linux (or other unixes):
Make should just work as is.

On Msys:
Once again, make should just work as long as you are running a bash 
shell (Cygwin has been tested)

On Windows (with bash shell and mingw runtime):
download and install a bash shell along with binutils for windows.
Before running mkae set the PLATFORM environment variable to 'Msys'

Implements the following:
1. xmalloc - A replacement for malloc. Provides, at runtime, a complete
   account of all the memory that has been malloc'ed with xmalloc. Can
   also be turned on/off at runtime. When off, xmalloc simply acts as
   a very thin wrapper around malloc and free. When turned on, xmalloc
   tracks all the memory allocations made, along with the source filename,
   the function and the line number of the call. In this mode, xmalloc
   can also, at callers request, dump all of the memory usage statistics
   and allocated blocklist to stderr.

   A signal handler is registered on first usage that dumps as much info
   as possible upon receipt of an access violation.

2. xstring - Common string routines for operations on null-terminated
   strings.  Provides safe methods to duplicate, concatenate, set, delete,
   convert (to number, float, etc), search, split, etc...  Works only
   on strings though, binary data might cause it to choke and crash.

3. xvector - A library providing a vector container service. Iteration
   can be performed with either a for loop (using the vector_get_size()
   function) or by calling vector_apply() with the appropriate predicate
   function. Only pointers should be stored in vectors, or PoDs that are
   less than a pointer in width. Other container types may utilise this
   container as their base unit of storage. A vector can grow and shrink 
   as elements are added to and removed from it.

4. xset - Implements containers of type set. Elements of xset are always
   unique. Equality is determined by naively comparing items of a set,
   so should item in a set be a pointer, note that no deep inspection
   will be performed. All elements of a set have to be less than or equal
   to a pointer width.

5. xtree - Library to create and traverse multiple-children nodes in a 
   tree. Each child node points to a single parent, while each parent
   node maintains an ordered list of all children nodes. The root of the
   tree has a null parent. Each node only stores a pointer to its payload.

6. xdict - Implementation of a dictionary array. A dictionary array is
   an array which is indexed not by its position but by a caller-provided
   name. xdict defaults to using strings (either xstrings or char *) as
   the lookup elements, but this behaviour can be changed to use almost
   any other datatype if the caller desires. xdict containers are always
   maintained in order, so insertion is O(log_n), while retrieval is
   O(n).

7. xsparse - Implementation of a sparse array (array which is mostly 
   empty). Base implementation uses xdict with the dictionary lookup
   element being unsigned integers.

8. xconfig - Library of routines to enable configuration management
   of options within a program, for example reading c/line args or
   configuration files, etc.
